oolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor`.\n```\n 620 | class PlotAccessor(PandasObject):\n 621 |     \"\"\"\n 622 |     Make plots of Series or DataFrame.\n 623 | \n 624 |     Uses the backend specified by the\n 625 |     option ``plotting.backend``. By default, matplotlib is used.\n 626 | \n 627 |     Parameters\n 628 |     ----------\n 629 |     data : Series or DataFrame\n 630 |         The object for which the method is called.\n 631 |     x : label or position, default None\n 632 |         Only used if data is a DataFrame.\n 633 |     y : label, position or list of label, positions, default None\n 634 |         Allows plotting of one column versus another. Only used if data is a\n 635 |         DataFrame.\n 636 |     kind : str\n 637 |         The kind of plot to produce:\n 638 | \n 639 |         - 'line' : line plot (default)\n 640 |         - 'bar' : vertical bar plot\n 641 |         - 'barh' : horizontal bar plot\n 642 |         - 'hist' : histogram\n 643 |         - 'box' : boxplot\n 644 |         - 'kde' : Kernel Density Estimation plot\n 645 |         - 'density' : same as 'kde'\n 646 |         - 'area' : area plot\n 647 |         - 'pie' : pie plot\n 648 |         - 'scatter' : scatter plot (DataFrame only)\n 649 |         - 'hexbin' : hexbin plot (DataFrame only)\n 650 |     ax : matplotlib axes object, default None\n 651 |         An axes of the current figure.\n 652 |     subplots : bool, default False\n 653 |         Make separate subplots for each column.\n 654 |     sharex : bool, default True if ax is None else False\n 655 |         In case ``subplots=True``, share x axis and set some x axis labels\n 656 |         to invisible; defaults to True if ax is None otherwise False if\n 657 |         an ax is passed in; Be aware, that passing in both an ax and\n 658 |         ``sharex=True`` will alter all x axis labels for all axis in a figure.\n 659 |     sharey : bool, default False\n 660 |         In case ``subplots=True``, share y axis and set some y axis labels to invisible.\n 661 |     layout : tuple, optional\n 662 |         (rows, columns) for the layout of subplots.\n 663 |     figsize : a tuple (width, height) in inches\n 664 |         Size of a figure object.\n 665 |     use_index : bool, default True\n 666 |         Use index as ticks for x axis.\n 667 |     title : str or list\n 668 |         Title to use for the plot. If a string is passed, print the string\n 669 |         at the top of the figure. If a list is passed and `subplots` is\n 670 |         True, print each item in the list above the corresponding subplot.\n 671 |     grid : bool, default None (matlab style default)\n 672 |         Axis grid lines.\n 673 |     legend : bool or {'reverse'}\n 674 |         Place legend on axis subplots.\n 675 |     style : list or dict\n 676 |         The matplotlib line style per column.\n 677 |     logx : bool or 'sym', default False\n 678 |         Use log scaling or symlog scaling on x axis.\n 679 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.25.0\n 680 | \n 681 |     logy : bool or 'sym' default False\n 682 |         Use log scaling or symlog scaling on y axis.\n 683 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.25.0\n 684 | \n 685 |     loglog : bool or 'sym', default False\n 686 |         Use log scaling or symlog scaling on both x and y axes.\n 687 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.25.0\n 688 | \n 689 |     xticks : sequence\n 690 |         Values to use for the xticks.\n 691 |     yticks : sequence\n 692 |         Values to use for the yticks.\n 693 |     xlim : 2-tuple/list\n 694 |         Set the x limits of the current axes.\n 695 |     ylim : 2-tuple/list\n 696 |         Set the y limits of the current axes.\n 697 |     xlabel : label, optional\n 698 |         Name to use for the xlabel on x-axis. Default uses index name as xlabel, or the\n 699 |         x-column name for planar plots.\n 700 | \n 701 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n 702 | \n 703 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.2.0\n 704 | \n 705 |            Now applicable to planar plots (`scatter`, `hexbin`).\n 706 | \n 707 |     ylabel : label, optional\n 708 |         Name to use for the ylabel on y-axis. Default will show no ylabel, or the\n 709 |         y-column name for planar plots.\n 710 | \n 711 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n 712 | \n 713 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.2.0\n 714 | \n 715 |            Now applicable to planar plots (`scatter`, `hexbin`).\n 716 | \n 717 |     rot : int, default None\n 718 |         Rotation for ticks (xticks for vertical, yticks for horizontal\n 719 |         plots).\n 720 |     fontsize : int, default None\n 721 |         Font size for xticks and yticks.\n 722 |     colormap : str or matplotlib colormap object, default None\n 723 |         Colormap to select colors from. If string, load colormap with that\n 724 |         name from matplotlib.\n 725 |     colorbar : bool, optional\n 726 |         If True, plot colorbar (only relevant for 'scatter' and 'hexbin'\n 727 |         plots).\n 728 |     position : float\n 729 |         Specify relative alignments for bar plot layout.\n 730 |         From 0 (left/bottom-end) to 1 (right/top-end). Default is 0.5\n 731 |         (center).\n 732 |     table : bool, Series or DataFrame, default False\n 733 |         If True, draw a table using the data in the DataFrame and the data\n 734 |         will be transposed to meet matplotlib's default layout.\n 735 |         If a Series or DataFrame is passed, use passed data to draw a\n 736 |         table.\n 737 |     yerr : DataFrame, Series, array-like, dict and str\n 738 |         See :ref:`Plotting with Error Bars <visualization.errorbars>` for\n 739 |         detail.\n 740 |     xerr : DataFrame, Series, array-like, dict and str\n 741 |         Equivalent to yerr.\n 742 |     stacked : bool, default False in line and bar plots, and True in area plot\n 743 |         If True, create stacked plot.\n 744 |     sort_columns : bool, default False\n 745 |         Sort column names to determine plot ordering.\n 746 |     secondary_y : bool or sequence, default False\n 747 |         Whether to plot on the secondary y-axis if a list/tuple, which\n 748 |         columns to plot on secondary y-axis.\n 749 |     mark_right : bool, default True\n 750 |         When using a secondary_y axis, automatically mark the column\n 751 |         labels with \"(right)\" in the legend.\n 752 |     include_bool : bool, default is False\n 753 |         If True, boolean values can be plotted.\n 754 |     backend : str, default None\n 755 |         Backend to use instead of the backend specified in the option\n 756 |         ``plotting.backend``. For instance, 'matplotlib'. Alternatively, to\n 757 |         specify the ``plotting.backend`` for the whole session, set\n 758 |         ``pd.options.plotting.backend``.\n 759 | \n 760 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0.0\n 761 | \n 762 |     **kwargs\n 763 |         Options to pass to matplotlib plotting method.\n 764 | \n 765 |     Returns\n 766 |     -------\n 767 |     :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n 768 |         If the backend is not the default matplotlib one, the return value\n 769 |         will be the object returned by the backend.\n 770 | \n 771 |     Notes\n 772 |     -----\n 773 |     - See matplotlib documentation online for more on this subject\n 774 |     - If `kind` = 'bar' or 'barh', you can specify relative alignments\n 775 |       for bar plot layout by `position` keyword.\n 776 |       From 0 (left/bottom-end) to 1 (right/top-end). Default is 0.5\n 777 |       (center)\n 778 |     \"\"\"\n 779 | \n 780 |     _common_kinds = (\"line\", \"bar\", \"barh\", \"kde\", \"density\", \"area\", \"hist\", \"box\")\n 781 |     _series_kinds = (\"pie\",)\n 782 |     _dataframe_kinds = (\"scatter\", \"hexbin\")\n 783 |     _kind_aliases = {\"density\": \"kde\"}\n 784 |     _all_kinds = _common_kinds + _series_kinds + _dataframe_kinds\n 785 | \n 786 |     def __init__(self, data) -> None:\n 787 |         self._parent = data\n 788 | \n 789 |     @staticmethod\n 790 |     def _get_call_args(backend_name, data, args, kwargs):\n 791 |         \"\"\"\n 792 |         This function makes calls to this accessor `__call__` method compatible\n 793 |         with the previous `SeriesPlotMethods.__call__` and\n 794 |         `DataFramePlotMethods.__call__`. Those had slightly different\n 795 |         signatures, since `DataFramePlotMethods` accepted `x` and `y`\n 796 |         parameters.\n 797 |         \"\"\"\n 798 |         if isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 799 |             arg_def = [\n 800 |                 (\"kind\", \"line\"),\n 801 |                 (\"ax\", None),\n 802 |                 (\"figsize\", None),\n 803 |                 (\"use_index\", True),\n 804 |                 (\"title\", None),\n 805 |                 (\"grid\", None),\n 806 |                 (\"legend\", False),\n 807 |                 (\"style\", None),\n 808 |                 (\"logx\", False),\n 809 |                 (\"logy\", False),\n 810 |                 (\"loglog\", False),\n 811 |                 (\"xticks\", None),\n 812 |                 (\"yticks\", None),\n 813 |                 (\"xlim\", None),\n 814 |                 (\"ylim\", None),\n 815 |                 (\"rot\", None),\n 816 |                 (\"fontsize\", None),\n 817 |                 (\"colormap\", None),\n 818 |                 (\"table\", False),\n 819 |                 (\"yerr\", None),\n 820 |                 (\"xerr\", None),\n 821 |                 (\"label\", None),\n 822 |                 (\"secondary_y\", False),\n 823 |                 (\"xlabel\", None),\n 824 |                 (\"ylabel\", None),\n 825 |             ]\n 826 |         elif isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 827 |             arg_def = [\n 828 |                 (\"x\", None),\n 829 |                 (\"y\", None),\n 830 |                 (\"kind\", \"line\"),\n 831 |                 (\"ax\", None),\n 832 |                 (\"subplots\", False),\n 833 |                 (\"sharex\", None),\n 834 |                 (\"sharey\", False),\n 835 |                 (\"layout\", None),\n 836 |                 (\"figsize\", None),\n 837 |                 (\"use_index\", True),\n 838 |                 (\"title\", None),\n 839 |                 (\"grid\", None),\n 840 |                 (\"legend\", True),\n 841 |                 (\"style\", None),\n 842 |                 (\"logx\", False),\n 843 |                 (\"logy\", False),\n 844 |                 (\"loglog\", False),\n 845 |                 (\"xticks\", None),\n 846 |                 (\"yticks\", None),\n 847 |                 (\"xlim\", None),\n 848 |                 (\"ylim\", None),\n 849 |                 (\"rot\", None),\n 850 |                 (\"fontsize\", None),\n 851 |                 (\"colormap\", None),\n 852 |                 (\"table\", False),\n 853 |                 (\"yerr\", None),\n 854 |                 (\"xerr\", None),\n 855 |                 (\"secondary_y\", False),\n 856 |                 (\"sort_columns\", False),\n 857 |                 (\"xlabel\", None),\n 858 |                 (\"ylabel\", None),\n 859 |             ]\n 860 |         else:\n 861 |             raise TypeError(\n 862 |                 f\"Called plot accessor for type {type(data).__name__}, \"\n 863 |                 \"expected Series or DataFrame\"\n 864 |             )\n 865 | \n 866 |         if args and isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 867 |             positional_args = str(args)[1:-1]\n 868 |             keyword_args = \", \".join(\n 869 |                 [f\"{name}={repr(value)}\" for (name, _), value in zip(arg_def, args)]\n 870 |             )\n 871 |             msg = (\n 872 |                 \"`Series.plot()` should not be called with positional \"\n 873 |                 \"arguments, only keyword arguments. The order of \"\n 874 |                 \"positional arguments will change in the future. \"\n 875 |                 f\"Use `Series.plot({keyword_args})` instead of \"\n 876 |                 f\"`Series.plot({positional_args})`.\"\n 877 |             )\n 878 |             raise TypeError(msg)\n 879 | \n 880 |         pos_args = {name: value for (name, _), value in zip(arg_def, args)}\n 881 |         if backend_name == \"pandas.plotting._matplotlib\":\n 882 |             kwargs = dict(arg_def, **pos_args, **kwargs)\n 883 |         else:\n 884 |             kwargs = dict(pos_args, **kwargs)\n 885 | \n 886 |         x = kwargs.pop(\"x\", None)\n 887 |         y = kwargs.pop(\"y\", None)\n 888 |         kind = kwargs.pop(\"kind\", \"line\")\n 889 |         return x, y, kind, kwargs\n 890 | \n 891 |     def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n 892 |         plot_backend = _get_plot_backend(kwargs.pop(\"backend\", None))\n 893 | \n 894 |         x, y, kind, kwargs = self._get_call_args(\n 895 |             plot_backend.__name__, self._parent, args, kwargs\n 896 |         )\n 897 | \n 898 |         kind = self._kind_aliases.get(kind, kind)\n 899 | \n 900 |         # when using another backend, get out of the way\n 901 |         if plot_backend.__name__ != \"pandas.plotting._matplotlib\":\n 902 |             return plot_backend.plot(self._parent, x=x, y=y, kind=kind, **kwargs)\n 903 | \n 904 |         if kind not in self._all_kinds:\n 905 |             raise ValueError(f\"{kind} is not a valid plot kind\")\n 906 | \n 907 |         # The original data structured can be transformed before passed to the\n 908 |         # backend. For example, for DataFrame is common to set the index as the\n 909 |         # `x` parameter, and return a Series with the parameter `y` as values.\n 910 |         data = self._parent.copy()\n 911 | \n 912 |         if isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 913 |             kwargs[\"reuse_plot\"] = True\n 914 | \n 915 |         if kind in self._dataframe_kinds:\n 916 |             if isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 917 |                 return plot_backend.plot(data, x=x, y=y, kind=kind, **kwargs)\n 918 |             else:\n 919 |                 raise ValueError(f\"plot kind {kind} can only be used for data frames\")\n 920 |         elif kind in self._series_kinds:\n 921 |             if isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 922 |                 if y is None and kwargs.get(\"subplots\") is False:\n 923 |                     raise ValueError(\n 924 |                         f\"{kind} requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\"\n 925 |                     )\n 926 |                 elif y is not None:\n 927 |                     if is_integer(y) and not data.columns.holds_integer():\n 928 |                         y = data.columns[y]\n 929 |                     # converted to series actually. copy to not modify\n 930 |                     data = data[y].copy()\n 931 |                     data.index.name = y\n 932 |         elif isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 933 |             data_cols = data.columns\n 934 |             if x is not None:\n 935 |                 if is_integer(x) and not data.columns.holds_integer():\n 936 |                     x = data_cols[x]\n 937 |                 elif not isinstance(data[x], ABCSeries):\n 938 |                     raise ValueError(\"x must be a label or position\")\n 939 |                 data = data.set_index(x)\n 940 |             if y is not None:\n 941 |                 # check if we have y as int or list of ints\n 942 |                 int_ylist = is_list_like(y) and all(is_integer(c) for c in y)\n 943 |                 int_y_arg = is_integer(y) or int_ylist\n 944 |                 if int_y_arg and not data.columns.holds_integer():\n 945 |                     y = data_cols[y]\n 946 | \n 947 |                 label_kw = kwargs[\"label\"] if \"label\" in kwargs else False\n 948 |                 for kw in [\"xerr\", \"yerr\"]:\n 949 |                     if kw in kwargs and (\n 950 |                         isinstance(kwargs[kw], str) or is_integer(kwargs[kw])\n 951 |                     ):\n 952 |                         try:\n 953 |                             kwargs[kw] = data[kwargs[kw]]\n 954 |                         except (IndexError, KeyError, TypeError):\n 955 |                             pass\n 956 | \n 957 |                 # don't overwrite\n 958 |                 data = data[y].copy()\n 959 | \n 960 |                 if isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 961 |                     label_name = label_kw or y\n 962 |                     data.name = label_name\n 963 |                 else:\n 964 |                     match = is_list_like(label_kw) and len(label_kw) == len(y)\n 965 |                     if label_kw and not match:\n 966 |                         raise ValueError(\n 967 |                             \"label should be list-like and same length as y\"\n 968 |                         )\n 969 |                     label_name = label_kw or data.columns\n 970 |                     data.columns = label_name\n 971 | \n 972 |         return plot_backend.plot(data, kind=kind, **kwargs)\n 973 | \n 974 |     __call__.__doc__ = __doc__\n 975 | \n 976 |     @Appender(\n 977 |         \"\"\"\n 978 |         See Also\n 979 |         --------\n 980 |         matplotlib.pyplot.plot : Plot y versus x as lines and/or markers.\n 981 | \n 982 |         Examples\n 983 |         --------\n 984 | \n 985 |         .. plot::\n 986 |             :context: close-figs\n 987 | \n 988 |             >>> s = pd.Series([1, 3, 2])\n 989 |             >>> s.plot.line()\n 990 |             <AxesSubplot:ylabel='Density'>\n 991 | \n 992 |         .. plot::\n 993 |             :context: close-figs\n 994 | \n 995 |             The following example shows the populations for some animals\n 996 |             over the years.\n 997 | \n 998 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n 999 |             ...    'pig': [20, 18, 489, 675, 1776],\n1000 |             ...    'horse': [4, 25, 281, 600, 1900]\n1001 |             ...    }, index=[1990, 1997, 2003, 2009, 2014])\n1002 |             >>> lines = df.plot.line()\n1003 | \n1004 |         .. plot::\n1005 |            :context: close-figs\n1006 | \n1007 |            An example with subplots, so an array of axes is returned.\n1008 | \n1009 |            >>> axes = df.plot.line(subplots=True)\n1010 |            >>> type(axes)\n1011 |            <class 'numpy.ndarray'>\n1012 | \n1013 |         .. plot::\n1014 |            :context: close-figs\n1015 | \n1016 |            Let's repeat the same example, but specifying colors for\n1017 |            each column (in this case, for each animal).\n1018 | \n1019 |            >>> axes = df.plot.line(\n1020 |            ...     subplots=True, color={\"pig\": \"pink\", \"horse\": \"#742802\"}\n1021 |            ... )\n1022 | \n1023 |         .. plot::\n1024 |             :context: close-figs\n1025 | \n1026 |             The following example shows the relationship between both\n1027 |             populations.\n1028 | \n1029 |             >>> lines = df.plot.line(x='pig', y='horse')\n1030 |         \"\"\"\n1031 |     )\n1032 |     @Substitution(kind=\"line\")\n1033 |     @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n1034 |     def line(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1035 |         \"\"\"\n1036 |         Plot Series or DataFrame as lines.\n1037 | \n1038 |         This function is useful to plot lines using DataFrame's values\n1039 |         as coordinates.\n1040 |         \"\"\"\n1041 |         return self(kind=\"line\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1042 | \n1043 |     @Appender(\n1044 |         \"\"\"\n1045 |         See Also\n1046 |         --------\n1047 |         DataFrame.plot.barh : Horizontal bar plot.\n1048 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1049 |         matplotlib.pyplot.bar : Make a bar plot with matplotlib.\n1050 | \n1051 |         Examples\n1052 |         --------\n1053 |         Basic plot.\n1054 | \n1055 |         .. plot::\n1056 |             :context: close-figs\n1057 | \n1058 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'lab':['A', 'B', 'C'], 'val':[10, 30, 20]})\n1059 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(x='lab', y='val', rot=0)\n1060 | \n1061 |         Plot a whole dataframe to a bar plot. Each column is assigned a\n1062 |         distinct color, and each row is nested in a group along the\n1063 |         horizontal axis.\n1064 | \n1065 |         .. plot::\n1066 |             :context: close-figs\n1067 | \n1068 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1069 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1070 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1071 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1072 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1073 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1074 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(rot=0)\n1075 | \n1076 |         Plot stacked bar charts for the DataFrame\n1077 | \n1078 |         .. plot::\n1079 |             :context: close-figs\n1080 | \n1081 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(stacked=True)\n1082 | \n1083 |         Instead of nesting, the figure can be split by column with\n1084 |         ``subplots=True``. In this case, a :class:`numpy.ndarray` of\n1085 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` are returned.\n1086 | \n1087 |         .. plot::\n1088 |             :context: close-figs\n1089 | \n1090 |             >>> axes = df.plot.bar(rot=0, subplots=True)\n1091 |             >>> axes[1].legend(loc=2)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1092 | \n1093 |         If you don't like the default colours, you can specify how you'd\n1094 |         like each column to be colored.\n1095 | \n1096 |         .. plot::\n1097 |             :context: close-figs\n1098 | \n1099 |             >>> axes = df.plot.bar(\n1100 |             ...     rot=0, subplots=True, color={\"speed\": \"red\", \"lifespan\": \"green\"}\n1101 |             ... )\n1102 |             >>> axes[1].legend(loc=2)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1103 | \n1104 |         Plot a single column.\n1105 | \n1106 |         .. plot::\n1107 |             :context: close-figs\n1108 | \n1109 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(y='speed', rot=0)\n1110 | \n1111 |         Plot only selected categories for the DataFrame.\n1112 | \n1113 |         .. plot::\n1114 |             :context: close-figs\n1115 | \n1116 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(x='lifespan', rot=0)\n1117 |     \"\"\"\n1118 |     )\n1119 |     @Substitution(kind=\"bar\")\n1120 |     @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n1121 |     def bar(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1122 |         \"\"\"\n1123 |         Vertical bar plot.\n1124 | \n1125 |         A bar plot is a plot that presents categorical data with\n1126 |         rectangular bars with lengths proportional to the values that they\n1127 |         represent. A bar plot shows comparisons among discrete categories. One\n1128 |         axis of the plot shows the specific categories being compared, and the\n1129 |         other axis represents a measured value.\n1130 |         \"\"\"\n1131 |         return self(kind=\"bar\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1132 | \n1133 |     @Appender(\n1134 |         \"\"\"\n1135 |         See Also\n1136 |         --------\n1137 |         DataFrame.plot.bar: Vertical bar plot.\n1138 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of DataFrame using matplotlib.\n1139 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar : Plot a vertical bar plot using matplotlib.\n1140 | \n1141 |         Examples\n1142 |         --------\n1143 |         Basic example\n1144 | \n1145 |         .. plot::\n1146 |             :context: close-figs\n1147 | \n1148 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'lab': ['A', 'B', 'C'], 'val': [10, 30, 20]})\n1149 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(x='lab', y='val')\n1150 | \n1151 |         Plot a whole DataFrame to a horizontal bar plot\n1152 | \n1153 |         .. plot::\n1154 |             :context: close-figs\n1155 | \n1156 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1157 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1158 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1159 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1160 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1161 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1162 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh()\n1163 | \n1164 |         Plot stacked barh charts for the DataFrame\n1165 | \n1166 |         .. plot::\n1167 |             :context: close-figs\n1168 | \n1169 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(stacked=True)\n1170 | \n1171 |         We can specify colors for each column\n1172 | \n1173 |         .. plot::\n1174 |             :context: close-figs\n1175 | \n1176 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(color={\"speed\": \"red\", \"lifespan\": \"green\"})\n1177 | \n1178 |         Plot a column of the DataFrame to a horizontal bar plot\n1179 | \n1180 |         .. plot::\n1181 |             :context: close-figs\n1182 | \n1183 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1184 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1185 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1186 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1187 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1188 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1189 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(y='speed')\n1190 | \n1191 |         Plot DataFrame versus the desired column\n1192 | \n1193 |         .. plot::\n1194 |             :context: close-figs\n1195 | \n1196 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1197 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1198 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1199 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1200 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1201 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1202 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(x='lifespan')\n1203 |     \"\"\"\n1204 |     )\n1205 |     @Substitution(kind=\"bar\")\n1206 |     @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n1207 |     def barh(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1208 |         \"\"\"\n1209 |         Make a horizontal bar plot.\n1210 | \n1211 |         A horizontal bar plot is a plot that presents quantitative data with\n1212 |         rectangular bars with lengths proportional to the values that they\n1213 |         represent. A bar plot shows comparisons among discrete categories. One\n1214 |         axis of the plot shows the specific categories being compared, and the\n1215 |         other axis represents a measured value.\n1216 |         \"\"\"\n1217 |         return self(kind=\"barh\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1218 | \n1219 |     def box(self, by=None, **kwargs):\n1220 |         r\"\"\"\n1221 |         Make a box plot of the DataFrame columns.\n1222 | \n1223 |         A box plot is a method for graphically depicting groups of numerical\n1224 |         data through their quartiles.\n1225 |         The box extends from the Q1 to Q3 quartile values of the data,\n1226 |         with a line at the median (Q2). The whiskers extend from the edges\n1227 |         of box to show the range of the data. The position of the whiskers\n1228 |         is set by default to 1.5*IQR (IQR = Q3 - Q1) from the edges of the\n1229 |         box. Outlier points are those past the end of the whiskers.\n1230 | \n1231 |         For further details see Wikipedia's\n1232 |         entry for `boxplot <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Box_plot>`__.\n1233 | \n1234 |         A consideration when using this chart is that the box and the whiskers\n1235 |         can overlap, which is very common when plotting small sets of data.\n1236 | \n1237 |         Parameters\n1238 |         ----------\n1239 |         by : str or sequence\n1240 |             Column in the DataFrame to group by.\n1241 | \n1242 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1243 | \n1244 |                Previously, `by` is silently ignore and makes no groupings\n1245 | \n1246 |         **kwargs\n1247 |             Additional keywords are documented in\n1248 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1249 | \n1250 |         Returns\n1251 |         -------\n1252 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n1253 | \n1254 |         See Also\n1255 |         --------\n1256 |         DataFrame.boxplot: Another method to draw a box plot.\n1257 |         Series.plot.box: Draw a box plot from a Series object.\n1258 |         matplotlib.pyplot.boxplot: Draw a box plot in matplotlib.\n1259 | \n1260 |         Examples\n1261 |         --------\n1262 |         Draw a box plot from a DataFrame with four columns of randomly\n1263 |         generated data.\n1264 | \n1265 |         .. plot::\n1266 |             :context: close-figs\n1267 | \n1268 |             >>> data = np.random.randn(25, 4)\n1269 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=list('ABCD'))\n1270 |             >>> ax = df.plot.box()\n1271 | \n1272 |         You can also generate groupings if you specify the `by` parameter (which\n1273 |         can take a column name, or a list or tuple of column names):\n1274 | \n1275 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1276 | \n1277 |         .. plot::\n1278 |             :context: close-figs\n1279 | \n1280 |             >>> age_list = [8, 10, 12, 14, 72, 74, 76, 78, 20, 25, 30, 35, 60, 85]\n1281 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"gender\": list(\"MMMMMMMMFFFFFF\"), \"age\": age_list})\n1282 |             >>> ax = df.plot.box(column=\"age\", by=\"gender\", figsize=(10, 8))\n1283 |         \"\"\"\n1284 |         return self(kind=\"box\", by=by, **kwargs)\n1285 | \n1286 |     def hist(self, by=None, bins=10, **kwargs):\n1287 |         \"\"\"\n1288 |         Draw one histogram of the DataFrame's columns.\n1289 | \n1290 |         A histogram is a representation of the distribution of data.\n1291 |         This function groups the values of all given Series in the DataFrame\n1292 |         into bins and draws all bins in one :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes`.\n1293 |         This is useful when the DataFrame's Series are in a similar scale.\n1294 | \n1295 |         Parameters\n1296 |         ----------\n1297 |         by : str or sequence, optional\n1298 |             Column in the DataFrame to group by.\n1299 | \n1300 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1301 | \n1302 |                Previously, `by` is silently ignore and makes no groupings\n1303 | \n1304 |         bins : int, default 10\n1305 |             Number of histogram bins to be used.\n1306 |         **kwargs\n1307 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1308 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1309 | \n1310 |         Returns\n1311 |         -------\n1312 |         class:`matplotlib.AxesSubplot`\n1313 |             Return a histogram plot.\n1314 | \n1315 |         See Also\n1316 |         --------\n1317 |         DataFrame.hist : Draw histograms per DataFrame's Series.\n1318 |         Series.hist : Draw a histogram with Series' data.\n1319 | \n1320 |         Examples\n1321 |         --------\n1322 |         When we roll a die 6000 times, we expect to get each value around 1000\n1323 |         times. But when we roll two dice and sum the result, the distribution\n1324 |         is going to be quite different. A histogram illustrates those\n1325 |         distributions.\n1326 | \n1327 |         .. plot::\n1328 |             :context: close-figs\n1329 | \n1330 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1331 |             ...     np.random.randint(1, 7, 6000),\n1332 |             ...     columns = ['one'])\n1333 |             >>> df['two'] = df['one'] + np.random.randint(1, 7, 6000)\n1334 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hist(bins=12, alpha=0.5)\n1335 | \n1336 |         A grouped histogram can be generated by providing the parameter `by` (which\n1337 |         can be a column name, or a list of column names):\n1338 | \n1339 |         .. plot::\n1340 |             :context: close-figs\n1341 | \n1342 |             >>> age_list = [8, 10, 12, 14, 72, 74, 76, 78, 20, 25, 30, 35, 60, 85]\n1343 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"gender\": list(\"MMMMMMMMFFFFFF\"), \"age\": age_list})\n1344 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hist(column=[\"age\"], by=\"gender\", figsize=(10, 8))\n1345 |         \"\"\"\n1346 |         return self(kind=\"hist\", by=by, bins=bins, **kwargs)\n1347 | \n1348 |     def kde(self, bw_method=None, ind=None, **kwargs):\n1349 |         \"\"\"\n1350 |         Generate Kernel Density Estimate plot using Gaussian kernels.\n1351 | \n1352 |         In statistics, `kernel density estimation`_ (KDE) is a non-parametric\n1353 |         way to estimate the probability density function (PDF) of a random\n1354 |         variable. This function uses Gaussian kernels and includes automatic\n1355 |         bandwidth determination.\n1356 | \n1357 |         .. _kernel density estimation:\n1358 |             https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_density_estimation\n1359 | \n1360 |         Parameters\n1361 |         ----------\n1362 |         bw_method : str, scalar or callable, optional\n1363 |             The method used to calculate the estimator bandwidth. This can be\n1364 |             'scott', 'silverman', a scalar constant or a callable.\n1365 |             If None (default), 'scott' is used.\n1366 |             See :class:`scipy.stats.gaussian_kde` for more information.\n1367 |         ind : NumPy array or int, optional\n1368 |             Evaluation points for the estimated PDF. If None (default),\n1369 |             1000 equally spaced points are used. If `ind` is a NumPy array, the\n1370 |             KDE is evaluated at the points passed. If `ind` is an integer,\n1371 |             `ind` number of equally spaced points are used.\n1372 |         **kwargs\n1373 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1374 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1375 | \n1376 |         Returns\n1377 |         -------\n1378 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or numpy.ndarray of them\n1379 | \n1380 |         See Also\n1381 |         --------\n1382 |         scipy.stats.gaussian_kde : Representation of a kernel-density\n1383 |             estimate using Gaussian kernels. This is the function used\n1384 |             internally to estimate the PDF.\n1385 | \n1386 |         Examples\n1387 |         --------\n1388 |         Given a Series of points randomly sampled from an unknown\n1389 |         distribution, estimate its PDF using KDE with automatic\n1390 |         bandwidth determination and plot the results, evaluating them at\n1391 |         1000 equally spaced points (default):\n1392 | \n1393 |         .. plot::\n1394 |             :context: close-figs\n1395 | \n1396 |             >>> s = pd.Series([1, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4, 5])\n1397 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde()\n1398 | \n1399 |         A scalar bandwidth can be specified. Using a small bandwidth value can\n1400 |         lead to over-fitting, while using a large bandwidth value may result\n1401 |         in under-fitting:\n1402 | \n1403 |         .. plot::\n1404 |             :context: close-figs\n1405 | \n1406 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde(bw_method=0.3)\n1407 | \n1408 |         .. plot::\n1409 |             :context: close-figs\n1410 | \n1411 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde(bw_method=3)\n1412 | \n1413 |         Finally, the `ind` parameter determines the evaluation points for the\n1414 |         plot of the estimated PDF:\n1415 | \n1416 |         .. plot::\n1417 |             :context: close-figs\n1418 | \n1419 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde(ind=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n1420 | \n1421 |         For DataFrame, it works in the same way:\n1422 | \n1423 |         .. plot::\n1424 |             :context: close-figs\n1425 | \n1426 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1427 |             ...     'x': [1, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4, 5],\n1428 |             ...     'y': [4, 4, 4.5, 5, 5.5, 6, 6],\n1429 |             ... })\n1430 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde()\n1431 | \n1432 |         A scalar bandwidth can be specified. Using a small bandwidth value can\n1433 |         lead to over-fitting, while using a large bandwidth value may result\n1434 |         in under-fitting:\n1435 | \n1436 |         .. plot::\n1437 |             :context: close-figs\n1438 | \n1439 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde(bw_method=0.3)\n1440 | \n1441 |         .. plot::\n1442 |             :context: close-figs\n1443 | \n1444 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde(bw_method=3)\n1445 | \n1446 |         Finally, the `ind` parameter determines the evaluation points for the\n1447 |         plot of the estimated PDF:\n1448 | \n1449 |         .. plot::\n1450 |             :context: close-figs\n1451 | \n1452 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde(ind=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\n1453 |         \"\"\"\n1454 |         return self(kind=\"kde\", bw_method=bw_method, ind=ind, **kwargs)\n1455 | \n1456 |     density = kde\n1457 | \n1458 |     def area(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1459 |         \"\"\"\n1460 |         Draw a stacked area plot.\n1461 | \n1462 |         An area plot displays quantitative data visually.\n1463 |         This function wraps the matplotlib area function.\n1464 | \n1465 |         Parameters\n1466 |         ----------\n1467 |         x : label or position, optional\n1468 |             Coordinates for the X axis. By default uses the index.\n1469 |         y : label or position, optional\n1470 |             Column to plot. By default uses all columns.\n1471 |         stacked : bool, default True\n1472 |             Area plots are stacked by default. Set to False to create a\n1473 |             unstacked plot.\n1474 |         **kwargs\n1475 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1476 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1477 | \n1478 |         Returns\n1479 |         -------\n1480 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or numpy.ndarray\n1481 |             Area plot, or array of area plots if subplots is True.\n1482 | \n1483 |         See Also\n1484 |         --------\n1485 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of DataFrame using matplotlib / pylab.\n1486 | \n1487 |         Examples\n1488 |         --------\n1489 |         Draw an area plot based on basic business metrics:\n1490 | \n1491 |         .. plot::\n1492 |             :context: close-figs\n1493 | \n1494 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1495 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3, 9, 10, 6],\n1496 |             ...     'signups': [5, 5, 6, 12, 14, 13],\n1497 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28, 62, 81, 50],\n1498 |             ... }, index=pd.date_range(start='2018/01/01', end='2018/07/01',\n1499 |             ...                        freq='M'))\n1500 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area()\n1501 | \n1502 |         Area plots are stacked by default. To produce an unstacked plot,\n1503 |         pass ``stacked=False``:\n1504 | \n1505 |         .. plot::\n1506 |             :context: close-figs\n1507 | \n1508 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(stacked=False)\n1509 | \n1510 |         Draw an area plot for a single column:\n1511 | \n1512 |         .. plot::\n1513 |             :context: close-figs\n1514 | \n1515 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(y='sales')\n1516 | \n1517 |         Draw with a different `x`:\n1518 | \n1519 |         .. plot::\n1520 |             :context: close-figs\n1521 | \n1522 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1523 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3],\n1524 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28],\n1525 |             ...     'day': [1, 2, 3],\n1526 |             ... })\n1527 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(x='day')\n1528 |         \"\"\"\n1529 |         return self(kind=\"area\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1530 | \n1531 |     def pie(self, **kwargs):\n1532 |         \"\"\"\n1533 |         Generate a pie plot.\n1534 | \n1535 |         A pie plot is a proportional representation of the numerical data in a\n1536 |         column. This function wraps :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.pie` for the\n1537 |         specified column. If no column reference is passed and\n1538 |         ``subplots=True`` a pie plot is drawn for each numerical column\n1539 |         independently.\n1540 | \n1541 |         Parameters\n1542 |         ----------\n1543 |         y : int or label, optional\n1544 |             Label or position of the column to plot.\n1545 |             If not provided, ``subplots=True`` argument must be passed.\n1546 |         **kwargs\n1547 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1548 | \n1549 |         Returns\n1550 |         -------\n1551 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or np.ndarray of them\n1552 |             A NumPy array is returned when `subplots` is True.\n1553 | \n1554 |         See Also\n1555 |         --------\n1556 |         Series.plot.pie : Generate a pie plot for a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...                   index=['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth'])\n1571 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(y='mass', figsize=(5, 5))\n1572 | \n1573 |         .. plot::\n1574 |             :context: close-figs\n1575 | \n1576 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(subplots=True, figsize=(11, 6))\n1577 |         \"\"\"\n1578 |         if (\n1579 |             isinstance(self._parent, ABCDataFrame)\n1580 |             and kwargs.get(\"y\", None) is None\n1581 |             and not kwargs.get(\"subplots\", False)\n1582 |         ):\n1583 |             raise ValueError(\"pie requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\")\n1584 |         return self(kind=\"pie\", **kwargs)\n1585 | \n1586 |     def scatter(self, x, y, s=None, c=None, **kwargs):\n1587 |         \"\"\"\n1588 |         Create a scatter plot with varying marker point size and color.\n1589 | \n1590 |         The coordinates of each point are defined by two dataframe columns and\n1591 |         filled circles are used to represent each point. This kind of plot is\n1592 |         useful to see complex correlations between two variables. Points could\n1593 |         be for instance natural 2D coordinates like longitude and latitude in\n1594 |         a map or, in general, any pair of metrics that can be plotted against\n1595 |         each other.\n1596 | \n1597 |         Parameters\n1598 |         ----------\n1599 |         x : int or str\n1600 |             The column name or column position to be used as horizontal\n1601 |             coordinates for each point.\n1602 |         y : int or str\n1603 |             The column name or column position to be used as vertical\n1604 |             coordinates for each point.\n1605 |         s : str, scalar or array-like, optional\n1606 |             The size of each point. Possible values are:\n1607 | \n1608 |             - A string with the name of the column to be used for marker's size.\n1609 | \n1610 |             - A single scalar so all points have the same size.\n1611 | \n1612 |             - A sequence of scalars, which will be used for each point's size\n1613 |               recursively. For instance, when passing [2,14] all points size\n1614 |               will be either 2 or 14, alternatively.\n1615 | \n1616 |               .. versionchanged:: 1.1.0\n1617 | \n1618 |         size : str, scalar or array-like, optional\n1619 |             Alias for s.\n1620 | \n1621 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n1622 | \n1623 |         c : str, int or array-like, optional\n1624 |             The color of each point. Possible values are:\n1625 | \n1626 |             - A single color string referred to by name, RGB or RGBA code,\n1627 |               for instance 'red' or '#a98d19'.\n1628 | \n1629 |             - A sequence of color strings referred to by name, RGB or RGBA\n1630 |               code, which will be used for each point's color recursively. For\n1631 |               instance ['green','yellow'] all points will be filled in green or\n1632 |               yellow, alternatively.\n1633 | \n1634 |             - A column name or position whose values will be used to color the\n1635 |               marker points according to a colormap.\n1636 |         color : str, int or array-like, optional\n1637 |             Alias for c.\n1638 | \n1639 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n1640 | \n1641 |         **kwargs\n1642 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1643 | \n1644 |         Returns\n1645 |         -------\n1646 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n1647 | \n1648 |         See Also\n1649 |         --------\n1650 |         matplotlib.pyplot.scatter : Scatter plot using multiple input data\n1651 |             formats.\n1652 | \n1653 |         Examples\n1654 |         --------\n1655 |         Let's see how to draw a scatter plot using coordinates from the values\n1656 |         in a DataFrame's columns.\n1657 | \n1658 |         .. plot::\n1659 |             :context: close-figs\n1660 | \n1661 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[5.1, 3.5, 0], [4.9, 3.0, 0], [7.0, 3.2, 1],\n1662 |             ...                    [6.4, 3.2, 1], [5.9, 3.0, 2]],\n1663 |             ...                   columns=['length', 'width', 'species'])\n1664 |             >>> ax1 = df.plot.scatter(x='length',\n1665 |             ...                       y='width',\n1666 |             ...                       c='DarkBlue')\n1667 | \n1668 |         And now with the color determined by a column as well.\n1669 | \n1670 |         .. plot::\n1671 |             :context: close-figs\n1672 | \n1673 |             >>> ax2 = df.plot.scatter(x='length',\n1674 |             ...                       y='width',\n1675 |             ...                       c='species',\n1676 |             ...                       colormap='viridis')\n1677 |         \"\"\"\n1678 |         size = kwargs.pop(\"size\", None)\n1679 |         if s is not None and size is not None:\n1680 |             raise TypeError(\"Specify exactly one of `s` and `size`\")\n1681 |         elif s is not None or size is not None:\n1682 |             kwargs[\"s\"] = s if s is not None else size\n1683 | \n1684 |         color = kwargs.pop(\"color\", None)\n1685 |         if c is not None and color is not None:\n1686 |             raise TypeError(\"Specify exactly one of `c` and `color`\")\n1687 |         elif c is not None or color is not None:\n1688 |             kwargs[\"c\"] = c if c is not None else color\n1689 | \n1690 |         return self(kind=\"scatter\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1691 | \n1692 |     def hexbin(self, x, y, C=None, reduce_C_function=None, gridsize=None, **kwargs):\n1693 |         \"\"\"\n1694 |         Generate a hexagonal binning plot.\n1695 | \n1696 |         Generate a hexagonal binning plot of `x` versus `y`. If `C` is `None`\n1697 |         (the default), this is a histogram of the number of occurrences\n1698 |         of the observations at ``(x[i], y[i])``.\n1699 | \n1700 |         If `C` is specified, specifies values at given coordinates\n1701 |         ``(x[i], y[i])``. These values are accumulated for each hexagonal\n1702 |         bin and then reduced according to `reduce_C_function`,\n1703 |         having as default the NumPy's mean function (:meth:`numpy.mean`).\n1704 |         (If `C` is specified, it must also be a 1-D sequence\n1705 |         of the same length as `x` and `y`, or a column label.)\n1706 | \n1707 |         Parameters\n1708 |         ----------\n1709 |         x : int or str\n1710 |             The column label or position for x points.\n1711 |         y : int or str\n1712 |             The column label or position for y points.\n1713 |         C : int or str, optional\n1714 |             The column label or position for the value of `(x, y)` point.\n1715 |         reduce_C_function : callable, default `np.mean`\n1716 |             Function of one argument that reduces all the values in a bin to\n1717 |             a single number (e.g. `np.mean`, `np.max`, `np.sum`, `np.std`).\n1718 |         gridsize : int or tuple of (int, int), default 100\n1719 |             The number of hexagons in the x-direction.\n1720 |             The corresponding number of hexagons in the y-direction is\n1721 |             chosen in a way that the hexagons are approximately regular.\n1722 |             Alternatively, gridsize can be a tuple with two elements\n1723 |             specifying the number of hexagons in the x-direction and the\n1724 |             y-direction.\n1725 |         **kwargs\n1726 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1727 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1728 | \n1729 |         Returns\n1730 |         -------\n1731 |         matplotlib.AxesSubplot\n1732 |             The matplotlib ``Axes`` on which the hexbin is plotted.\n1733 | \n1734 |         See Also\n1735 |         --------\n1736 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1737 |         matplotlib.pyplot.hexbin : Hexagonal binning plot using matplotlib,\n1738 |             the matplotlib function that is used under the hood.\n1739 | \n1740 |         Examples\n1741 |         --------\n1742 |         The following examples are generated with random data from\n1743 |         a normal distribution.\n1744 | \n1745 |         .. plot::\n1746 |             :context: close-figs\n1747 | \n1748 |             >>> n = 10000\n1749 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'x': np.random.randn(n),\n1750 |             ...                    'y': np.random.randn(n)})\n1751 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hexbin(x='x', y='y', gridsize=20)\n1752 | \n1753 |         The next example uses `C` and `np.sum` as `reduce_C_function`.\n1754 |         Note that `'observations'` values ranges from 1 to 5 but the result\n1755 |         plot shows values up to more than 25. This is because of the\n1756 |         `reduce_C_function`.\n1757 | \n1758 |         .. plot::\n1759 |             :context: close-figs\n1760 | \n1761 |             >>> n = 500\n1762 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1763 |             ...     'coord_x': np.random.uniform(-3, 3, size=n),\n1764 |             ...     'coord_y': np.random.uniform(30, 50, size=n),\n1765 |             ...     'observations': np.random.randint(1,5, size=n)\n1766 |             ...     })\n1767 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hexbin(x='coord_x',\n1768 |             ...                     y='coord_y',\n1769 |             ...                     C='observations',\n1770 |             ...                     reduce_C_function=np.sum,\n1771 |             ...                     gridsize=10,\n1772 |             ...                     cmap=\"viridis\")\n1773 |         \"\"\"\n1774 |         if reduce_C_function is not None:\n1775 |             kwargs[\"reduce_C_function\"] = reduce_C_function\n1776 |         if gridsize is not None:\n1777 |             kwargs[\"gridsize\"] = gridsize\n1778 | \n1779 |         return self(kind=\"hexbin\", x=x, y=y, C=C, **kwargs)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis of the code and the feature request, here are the key locations that need to be modified to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nline: 372-394\nfunction: MPLPlot._setup_subplots\ndescription: Main function that creates subplots. Needs to be modified to handle grouping of columns\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nline: 1034-1041\nfunction: PlotAccessor.line\ndescription: The plotting accessor method that processes plot arguments\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nline: 780-784\ndescription: Defines common plotting parameters and their documentation\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py\nline: 132-312\nfunction: create_subplots\ndescription: Utility function that creates the actual subplot structure\n\npandas/plotting/_core.py\nline: 652-653\ndescription: Documentation of the subplots parameter in PlotAccessor class\n```\n\nExplanation of why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._setup_subplots` is the primary location where the subplot creation happens. Currently, it creates one subplot per column (line 375: `naxes = self.nseries`). To implement column grouping, this function needs to be modified to:\n   - Handle the case of grouped columns differently from individual columns\n   - Calculate the correct number of axes needed based on the grouping specification\n   - Properly arrange the columns within each group\n\n2. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:PlotAccessor.line` is the main entry point for plotting operations. This function needs to be updated to:\n   - Accept the new grouping parameter format (list of tuples)\n   - Process the grouping information to determine which columns should be grouped together\n   - Pass the appropriate grouping information to the plotting logic\n\n3. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` lines 780-784 contain the parameter documentation for the subplots functionality. This section needs to be updated to document the new grouping parameter format.\n\n4. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots` is a utility function that actually creates the subplot structure. While it doesn't need direct modifications, it's important to understand its implementation as it's used by `_setup_subplots` to create the actual axes.\n\n5. The documentation in `pandas/plotting/_core.py` lines 652-653 in the PlotAccessor class needs to be updated to describe the new parameter format and behavior.\n\nThese locations represent form the core of what needs to be modified to implement the requested feature. The main changes would focus on how columns are grouped and plotted rather than adding entirely new functionality, as suggested in the issue description.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 175443, "completion_tokens": 845}, "time": 155.30936813354492}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-30327", "found_files": [["sklearn/utils/_tags.py", "sklearn/base.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/exceptions.py:EstimatorCheckFailedWarning", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:default_tags", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_InstancesOf", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_check_device_cpu", "sklearn/decomposition/_truncated_svd.py:TruncatedSVD", "sklearn/cluster/_birch.py:_CFSubcluster", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_accept_device_cpu", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:Version", "sklearn/mixture/_base.py:BaseMixture", "sklearn/feature_selection/_sequential.py:SequentialFeatureSelector", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffException", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "sklearn/ensemble/_bagging.py:BaseBagging", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/exceptions.py:EstimatorCheckFailedWarning.__str__", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark.make_scorers", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:default_tags", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_InstancesOf.is_satisfied_by", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint.is_satisfied_by", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_check_device_cpu", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint.__str__", "sklearn/decomposition/_truncated_svd.py:TruncatedSVD.__sklearn_tags__", "sklearn/cluster/_birch.py:_CFSubcluster.update", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_accept_device_cpu", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:Version.__repr__", "sklearn/mixture/_base.py:BaseMixture._get_parameters", "sklearn/feature_selection/_sequential.py:SequentialFeatureSelector._get_support_mask", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffException.__str__", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "sklearn/ensemble/_bagging.py:BaseBagging._parallel_args", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator.__sklearn_tags__"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the analysis of the issue and codebase exploration, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 246-252\nfunction: default_tags\nnote: Need to modify to handle _get_tags and _more_tags deprecation warning\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 293-334\nfunction: get_tags\nnote: Main function that needs modification to handle old and new tag conversions\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 519-583\nfunction: _to_old_tags\nnote: Implementation of old tag conversion\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 457-515\nfunction: _to_new_tags\nnote: Implementation of new tag conversion\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 391-399\nfunction: BaseEstimator.__sklearn_tags__\nnote: Reference implementation for tag handling\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "90a51c12d6159f72ab8990ea1a3ff835861ec81d", "problem_statement": "MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\r\n\n", "patch": "diff --git a/sklearn/base.py b/sklearn/base.py\nindex d646f8d3e56bf..2c82cf05a6c5a 100644\n--- a/sklearn/base.py\n+++ b/sklearn/base.py\n@@ -389,6 +389,33 @@ def __setstate__(self, state):\n         except AttributeError:\n             self.__dict__.update(state)\n \n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _more_tags(self):\n+        \"\"\"This code should never be reached since our `get_tags` will fallback on\n+        `__sklearn_tags__` implemented below. We keep it for backward compatibility.\n+        It is tested in `test_base_estimator_more_tags` in\n+        `sklearn/utils/testing/test_tags.py`.\"\"\"\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, default_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_more_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+        return _to_old_tags(default_tags(self))\n+\n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _get_tags(self):\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, get_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_get_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+\n+        return _to_old_tags(get_tags(self))\n+\n     def __sklearn_tags__(self):\n         return Tags(\n             estimator_type=None,\ndiff --git a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\nindex ccbc9d2438268..1ba1913c37234 100644\n--- a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n+++ b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import warnings\n+from collections import OrderedDict\n from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n+from itertools import chain\n \n from .fixes import _dataclass_args\n \n@@ -290,6 +292,71 @@ def default_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     )\n \n \n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _find_tags_provider(estimator, warn=True):\n+    \"\"\"Find the tags provider for an estimator.\n+\n+    Parameters\n+    ----------\n+    estimator : estimator object\n+        The estimator to find the tags provider for.\n+\n+    warn : bool, default=True\n+        Whether to warn if the tags provider is not found.\n+\n+    Returns\n+    -------\n+    tag_provider : str\n+        The tags provider for the estimator. Can be one of:\n+        - \"_get_tags\": to use the old tags infrastructure\n+        - \"__sklearn_tags__\": to use the new tags infrastructure\n+    \"\"\"\n+    mro_model = type(estimator).mro()\n+    tags_mro = OrderedDict()\n+    for klass in mro_model:\n+        tags_provider = []\n+        if \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_more_tags\")\n+        if \"_get_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_get_tags\")\n+        if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"__sklearn_tags__\")\n+        tags_mro[klass.__name__] = tags_provider\n+\n+    all_providers = set(chain.from_iterable(tags_mro.values()))\n+    if \"__sklearn_tags__\" not in all_providers:\n+        # default on the old tags infrastructure\n+        return \"_get_tags\"\n+\n+    tag_provider = \"__sklearn_tags__\"\n+    for klass in tags_mro:\n+        has_get_or_more_tags = any(\n+            provider in tags_mro[klass] for provider in (\"_get_tags\", \"_more_tags\")\n+        )\n+        has_sklearn_tags = \"__sklearn_tags__\" in tags_mro[klass]\n+\n+        if tags_mro[klass] and tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":  # is it empty\n+            if has_get_or_more_tags and not has_sklearn_tags:\n+                # Case where a class does not implement __sklearn_tags__ and we fallback\n+                # to _get_tags. We should therefore warn for implementing\n+                # __sklearn_tags__.\n+                tag_provider = \"_get_tags\"\n+                break\n+\n+    if warn and tag_provider == \"_get_tags\":\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            f\"The {estimator.__class__.__name__} or classes from which it inherits \"\n+            \"use `_get_tags` and `_more_tags`. Please define the \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` method, or inherit from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` \"\n+            \"and/or other appropriate mixins such as `sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, `sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`. From scikit-learn 1.7, not defining \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` will raise an error.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+    return tag_provider\n+\n+\n def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     \"\"\"Get estimator tags.\n \n@@ -316,19 +383,201 @@ def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n         The estimator tags.\n     \"\"\"\n \n-    if hasattr(estimator, \"__sklearn_tags__\"):\n+    tag_provider = _find_tags_provider(estimator)\n+\n+    if tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":\n         tags = estimator.__sklearn_tags__()\n     else:\n-        warnings.warn(\n-            f\"Estimator {estimator} has no __sklearn_tags__ attribute, which is \"\n-            \"defined in `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator`. This will raise an error in \"\n-            \"scikit-learn 1.8. Please define the __sklearn_tags__ method, or inherit \"\n-            \"from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` and other appropriate mixins such as \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, `sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and `sklearn.base.ClusterMixin`, and \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`.\",\n-            category=FutureWarning,\n+        # TODO(1.7): Remove this branch of the code\n+        # Let's go through the MRO and patch each class implementing _more_tags\n+        sklearn_tags_provider = {}\n+        more_tags_provider = {}\n+        class_order = []\n+        for klass in reversed(type(estimator).mro()):\n+            if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+                sklearn_tags_provider[klass] = klass.__sklearn_tags__(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+            elif \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+                more_tags_provider[klass] = klass._more_tags(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+\n+        # Find differences between consecutive in the case of __sklearn_tags__\n+        # inheritance\n+        sklearn_tags_diff = {}\n+        items = list(sklearn_tags_provider.items())\n+        for current_item, next_item in zip(items[:-1], items[1:]):\n+            current_name, current_tags = current_item\n+            next_name, next_tags = next_item\n+            current_tags = _to_old_tags(current_tags)\n+            next_tags = _to_old_tags(next_tags)\n+\n+            # Compare tags and store differences\n+            diff = {}\n+            for key in current_tags:\n+                if current_tags[key] != next_tags[key]:\n+                    diff[key] = next_tags[key]\n+\n+            sklearn_tags_diff[next_name] = diff\n+\n+        tags = {}\n+        for klass in class_order:\n+            if klass in sklearn_tags_diff:\n+                tags.update(sklearn_tags_diff[klass])\n+            elif klass in more_tags_provider:\n+                tags.update(more_tags_provider[klass])\n+\n+        tags = _to_new_tags(\n+            {**_to_old_tags(default_tags(estimator)), **tags}, estimator\n         )\n-        tags = default_tags(estimator)\n \n     return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _safe_tags(estimator, key=None):\n+    warnings.warn(\n+        \"The `_safe_tags` function is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+        \"1.7. Use the public `get_tags` function instead and make sure to implement \"\n+        \"the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+        category=FutureWarning,\n+    )\n+    tags = _to_old_tags(get_tags(estimator))\n+\n+    if key is not None:\n+        if key not in tags:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                f\"The key {key} is not defined for the class \"\n+                f\"{estimator.__class__.__name__}.\"\n+            )\n+        return tags[key]\n+    return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_new_tags(old_tags, estimator=None):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    input_tags = InputTags(\n+        one_d_array=\"1darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_array=\"2darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        three_d_array=\"3darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        sparse=\"sparse\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        categorical=\"categorical\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        string=\"string\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        dict=\"dict\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_X\"],\n+        allow_nan=old_tags[\"allow_nan\"],\n+        pairwise=old_tags[\"pairwise\"],\n+    )\n+    target_tags = TargetTags(\n+        required=old_tags[\"requires_y\"],\n+        one_d_labels=\"1dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_labels=\"2dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_y\"],\n+        multi_output=old_tags[\"multioutput\"] or old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+        single_output=not old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+    )\n+    if estimator is not None and (\n+        hasattr(estimator, \"transform\") or hasattr(estimator, \"fit_transform\")\n+    ):\n+        transformer_tags = TransformerTags(\n+            preserves_dtype=old_tags[\"preserves_dtype\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        transformer_tags = None\n+    estimator_type = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None)\n+    if estimator_type == \"classifier\":\n+        classifier_tags = ClassifierTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_class=not old_tags[\"binary_only\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        classifier_tags = None\n+    if estimator_type == \"regressor\":\n+        regressor_tags = RegressorTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        regressor_tags = None\n+    return Tags(\n+        estimator_type=estimator_type,\n+        target_tags=target_tags,\n+        transformer_tags=transformer_tags,\n+        classifier_tags=classifier_tags,\n+        regressor_tags=regressor_tags,\n+        input_tags=input_tags,\n+        array_api_support=old_tags[\"array_api_support\"],\n+        no_validation=old_tags[\"no_validation\"],\n+        non_deterministic=old_tags[\"non_deterministic\"],\n+        requires_fit=old_tags[\"requires_fit\"],\n+        _skip_test=old_tags[\"_skip_test\"],\n+    )\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_old_tags(new_tags):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    if new_tags.classifier_tags:\n+        binary_only = not new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_class\n+        multilabel_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        binary_only = False\n+        multilabel_clf = False\n+        poor_score_clf = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.regressor_tags:\n+        multilabel_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        multilabel_reg = False\n+        poor_score_reg = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.transformer_tags:\n+        preserves_dtype = new_tags.transformer_tags.preserves_dtype\n+    else:\n+        preserves_dtype = [\"float64\"]\n+\n+    tags = {\n+        \"allow_nan\": new_tags.input_tags.allow_nan,\n+        \"array_api_support\": new_tags.array_api_support,\n+        \"binary_only\": binary_only,\n+        \"multilabel\": multilabel_clf or multilabel_reg,\n+        \"multioutput\": new_tags.target_tags.multi_output,\n+        \"multioutput_only\": (\n+            not new_tags.target_tags.single_output and new_tags.target_tags.multi_output\n+        ),\n+        \"no_validation\": new_tags.no_validation,\n+        \"non_deterministic\": new_tags.non_deterministic,\n+        \"pairwise\": new_tags.input_tags.pairwise,\n+        \"preserves_dtype\": preserves_dtype,\n+        \"poor_score\": poor_score_clf or poor_score_reg,\n+        \"requires_fit\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+        \"requires_positive_X\": new_tags.input_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"requires_y\": new_tags.target_tags.required,\n+        \"requires_positive_y\": new_tags.target_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"_skip_test\": new_tags._skip_test,\n+        \"stateless\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+    }\n+    X_types = []\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.one_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"1darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.two_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"2darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.three_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"3darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.sparse:\n+        X_types.append(\"sparse\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.categorical:\n+        X_types.append(\"categorical\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.string:\n+        X_types.append(\"string\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.dict:\n+        X_types.append(\"dict\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.one_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"1dlabels\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.two_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"2dlabels\")\n+    tags[\"X_types\"] = X_types\n+    return tags\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 174055, "completion_tokens": 447}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described execution flow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described execution flow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Clarify the Purpose of the Issue\n    - If expanding capabilities: Identify where and how are the new behavior, fields, or modules incorporated.\n- Reconstruct the execution flow\n    - Identify main entry points triggering the feature described in the issue.\n    - Trace function calls, class interactions, and sequences of events.\n    - Identify potential relevant points in the code workflow of the codebase.\n    - Use the unique artifacts provided to you to complement your understanding of the execution flow, or use as a hint.\n    Important: Keep the reconstructed flow focused on the problem, avoiding irrelevant details.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\n\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental context information:\n\nYou will receive supplemental context for the current task. Use it to better understand the issue and to localize the relevant code more accurately.\n\nTreat this context as additional evidence, not as the final answer.\n- It may contain noise.\n- It may be incomplete.\n- You should carefully analyze it and decide how to use it when searching for relevant code locations.\n\nDepending on the settings, the supplemental context may include:\n1. Unique artifacts\n2. Unique dependency tree\n\n## How to use this supplemental context\n- Use it to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself.\n\n## What the provided information means\nAssume the issue sent to you is a feature request requesting feature F.\n\n1. Unique artifacts\n- The unique artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis.\n- For the requested feature described in the issue text, two Python scripts, A.py and B.py, describing the same process are executed and traced.\n- The only difference is that A.py is executed with feature F enabled, while B.py is executed with the feature disabled.\n- By comparing the two tracing results, the overlapping part between the trace for A.py and B.py is subtracted.\n- The remaining artifacts are the unique artifacts that appear only in A.py.\n- These unique artifacts are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree\n- The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies