        \"\"\"Return True if a and b are structurally the same, else False.\n2165 |         If `approx` is supplied, it will be used to test whether two\n2166 |         numbers are the same or not. By default, only numbers of the\n2167 |         same type will compare equal, so S.Half != Float(0.5).\n2168 | \n2169 |         Examples\n2170 |         ========\n2171 | \n2172 |         In SymPy (unlike Python) two numbers do not compare the same if they are\n2173 |         not of the same type:\n2174 | \n2175 |         >>> from sympy import S\n2176 |         >>> 2.0 == S(2)\n2177 |         False\n2178 |         >>> 0.5 == S.Half\n2179 |         False\n2180 | \n2181 |         By supplying a function with which to compare two numbers, such\n2182 |         differences can be ignored. e.g. `equal_valued` will return True\n2183 |         for decimal numbers having a denominator that is a power of 2,\n2184 |         regardless of precision.\n2185 | \n2186 |         >>> from sympy import Float\n2187 |         >>> from sympy.core.numbers import equal_valued\n2188 |         >>> (S.Half/4).is_same(Float(0.125, 1), equal_valued)\n2189 |         True\n2190 |         >>> Float(1, 2).is_same(Float(1, 10), equal_valued)\n2191 |         True\n2192 | \n2193 |         But decimals without a power of 2 denominator will compare\n2194 |         as not being the same.\n2195 | \n2196 |         >>> Float(0.1, 9).is_same(Float(0.1, 10), equal_valued)\n2197 |         False\n2198 | \n2199 |         But arbitrary differences can be ignored by supplying a function\n2200 |         to test the equivalence of two numbers:\n2201 | \n2202 |         >>> import math\n2203 |         >>> Float(0.1, 9).is_same(Float(0.1, 10), math.isclose)\n2204 |         True\n2205 | \n2206 |         Other objects might compare the same even though types are not the\n2207 |         same. This routine will only return True if two expressions are\n2208 |         identical in terms of class types.\n2209 | \n2210 |         >>> 