        -------\n2423 |         None or str\n2424 |             If path_or_buf is None, returns the resulting json format as a\n2425 |             string. Otherwise returns None.\n2426 | \n2427 |         See Also\n2428 |         --------\n2429 |         read_json : Convert a JSON string to pandas object.\n2430 | \n2431 |         Notes\n2432 |         -----\n2433 |         The behavior of ``indent=0`` varies from the stdlib, which does not\n2434 |         indent the output but does insert newlines. Currently, ``indent=0``\n2435 |         and the default ``indent=None`` are equivalent in pandas, though this\n2436 |         may change in a future release.\n2437 | \n2438 |         ``orient='table'`` contains a 'pandas_version' field under 'schema'.\n2439 |         This stores the version of `pandas` used in the latest revision of the\n2440 |         schema.\n2441 | \n2442 |         Examples\n2443 |         --------\n2444 |         >>> from json import loads, dumps\n2445 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n2446 |         ...     [[\"a\", \"b\"], [\"c\", \"d\"]],\n2447 |         ...     index=[\"row 1\", \"row 2\"],\n2448 |         ...     columns=[\"col 1\", \"col 2\"],\n2449 |         ... )\n2450 | \n2451 |         >>> result = df.to_json(orient=\"split\")\n2452 |         >>> parsed = loads(result)\n2453 |         >>> dumps(parsed, indent=4)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2454 |         {{\n2455 |             \"columns\": [\n2456 |                 \"col 1\",\n2457 |                 \"col 2\"\n2458 |             ],\n2459 |             \"index\": [\n2460 |                 \"row 1\",\n2461 |                 \"row 2\"\n2462 |             ],\n2463 |             \"data\": [\n2464 |                 [\n2465 |                     \"a\",\n2466 |                     \"b\"\n2467 |                 ],\n2468 |                 [\n2469 |                     \"c\",\n2470 |                     \"d\"\n2471 |                 ]\n2472 |             ]\n2473 |         }}\n2474 | \n2475 |         Encoding/dec